    {
      "id": "UgwtKyaRqEV9y8vN_Zd4AaABAg",
      "author": "@UchiesandChromehearts",
      "authorImage": "https://yt3.ggpht.com/5c5NoUrZ7S3JDlvhvsl6wjrqin-aA4fsDJIYqx-Iw2gi7inVPpdu4wsxKT_LU2POfI7rmjbg=s48-c-k-c0x00ffffff-no-rj",
      "text": "San Juan isn't the oldest city in the Americas that will be Santo Domingo founded in 1492. Instead, it's capital, San Juan, Puerto Rico, is widely recognized as the second-oldest European-established capital city in the Americas after Santo Domingo.\n\nSanto Domingo is often called the \"cradle of European civilization\" (or Western colonial civilization) in the Americas because it is the oldest continuously inhabited European settlement in the New World. Established on the south coast of Hispaniola at the mouth of the Ozama River in the late 1490s (1496–1498), it served as the first capital of Spanish colonial rule and the launchpad for expeditions across the Americas.\n\nNotable \"Firsts\" in the Americas\n\n• First Cathedral: Catedral de Santa María la Menor (begun in the early 16th century).\n• First Fortress: Fortaleza Ozama, built along the Ozama River to defend the settlement.\n• First University: Autonomous University of Santo Domingo (founded in 1538).\n• Colonial Landmarks: The Alcázar de Colón (residence of Diego Columbus) and early civic/religious institutions established during Spanish rule.",
      "likeCount": 0,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T23:14:39Z",
      "replyCount": 0,
      "replies": []
    },
